if NROS_RMW_CYCLONEDDS

config NROS_CYCLONE_DOMAIN_ID
    int "DDS domain ID"
    default NROS_DOMAIN_ID
    range 0 232
    help
      DDS domain ID passed to dds_create_domain_with_rawconfig at init.
      Must match the ROS 2 peer's ROS_DOMAIN_ID for discovery. Defaults to
      NROS_DOMAIN_ID so the two knobs cannot split-brain (issue 0161: the
      phase-180 split silently pinned Cyclone to 0 while the 177.37 fixture
      driver baked per-role-set NROS_DOMAIN_ID values); set it explicitly
      only when the DDS domain must intentionally differ from the ROS one.

config NROS_CYCLONE_CONFIG_XML
    string "Cyclone raw config XML (empty = default)"
    default ""
    help
      Optional Cyclone DDS configuration XML blob baked at build time
      (Zephyr has no filesystem to read from at runtime, and native_sim
      has no usable environment for CYCLONEDDS_URI — issue 0367). Leave
      empty to use the platform's baked profile; set this to override
      transport / discovery / tracing settings wholesale (the blob
      REPLACES the baked profile — carry over its Sizing/Threads
      sections). Use single-quoted XML attributes
      (Address='127.0.0.1') — Kconfig strings and escaped double
      quotes do not mix.

endif # NROS_RMW_CYCLONEDDS

# =============================================================================
# Zenoh-pico Feature Configuration
# =============================================================================
# These options control which zenoh-pico features are compiled in.
# They map directly to Z_FEATURE_* compile definitions.
# Defaults are set for typical ROS 2 usage; disable unused features
# to reduce code size on constrained targets.

if NROS_RMW_ZENOH

config NROS_ZENOH_MULTI_THREAD
    bool "Zenoh-pico multithreading"
    default y
    # sched_get_priority_min/max are defined in lib/posix/options/sched.c,
    # which is compiled only under POSIX_PRIORITY_SCHEDULING. CONFIG_POSIX_API
    # does NOT select it -- it is its own option group -- so declaring POSIX_API
    # gets the declarations from <sched.h> and no definitions, and the failure
    # lands at LINK time in zpico.c rather than as a missing-config diagnostic.
    #
    # The dependency is stated here, on the symbol that both spawns the tasks
    # and compiles the call: zpico_set_task_config's body is behind
    # Z_FEATURE_MULTI_THREAD, which this symbol maps to. It cannot be stated in
    # zpico.c instead -- zpico-sys/build.rs forwards no CONFIG_* defines to the
    # shim, so an #ifdef there would read as permanently false and silently
    # discard the priority the user configured, which is the failure issue 0626
    # set out to remove.
    select POSIX_PRIORITY_SCHEDULING
    help
      Enable multithreading in zenoh-pico. Uses Zephyr threads for
      background read/lease tasks. Required for non-blocking operation.
      Maps to Z_FEATURE_MULTI_THREAD.

config NROS_ZENOH_SOCKET_TIMEOUT_MS
    int "zenoh-pico socket recv timeout (ms)"
    default 100
    help
      SO_RCVTIMEO for the zenoh-pico session socket (Z_CONFIG_SOCKET_TIMEOUT).
      Zephyr's zsock layer serializes send/recv on a per-fd mutex, so the
      blocking read task holds the socket for a full recv window between
      inbound packets and EVERY tx (declare, keepalive, publish, reply)
      waits for that window — total tx throughput is capped at roughly one
      send per window (issues 0129/0139). The 100 ms default suits ~1-10 Hz
      demo lanes; high-rate publishers (e.g. RT-tier systems at 100 Hz)
      must lower this (5 ms => ~200 tx windows/s) at the cost of more
      read-task wakeups.

config NROS_ZEPHYR_TASK_STACK_SIZE
    int "nano-ros Zephyr task stack size (bytes, 0 = inherit MAIN_STACK_SIZE)"
    default 0
    help
      NROS_ZEPHYR_STACK_SIZE in zephyr/nros_platform_zephyr_shims.c — the stack
      given to every task nano-ros creates on Zephyr, which on a zenoh image is
      the read task, the lease task and the tx-flush task. It defaults to
      CONFIG_MAIN_STACK_SIZE, which couples two unrelated budgets: raising
      main's stack because MAIN needs it also multiplies into
      NROS_ZEPHYR_TASK_SLOTS stacks of the same size.

      On a 320 KiB part that is a link failure rather than a tuning nuisance.
      Measured on mr_canhubk3/s32k344: MAIN_STACK_SIZE 8192 -> 16384 overflowed
      RAM by 21588 bytes, entirely because four zenoh stacks grew with it.

      It also makes CONFIG_HW_STACK_PROTECTION unusable on a tight image — the
      guard region pushes main over at boot, so the one tool that names an
      overflowing thread cannot be enabled.

      0 keeps upstream behaviour. Set it to size the zenoh tasks for their own
      deepest call chain, which is the expiry teardown, independently of main.

config NROS_FAULT_LOG
    bool "Record a post-mortem fault record that survives reset"
    default n
    help
      Override `k_sys_fatal_error_handler` to capture the fault reason, the
      faulting PC/LR/PSR and the thread NAME into a `.noinit` record, and print
      it on the next boot.

      This exists because a debugger is not a passive instrument on this class
      of board. Attaching pyocd's RTT to read the console halts the core often
      enough that a zenoh session misses its keepalives and expires, and issue
      0879 then makes that permanent. A fault you can only see by attaching a
      probe is a fault you have partly caused.

      The handler writes a few stores and nothing else: it runs with interrupts
      off, on a stack that may be the one that overflowed.

      "Which thread" is the whole question for a stack overflow, so enable
      CONFIG_THREAD_NAME alongside this or the record says "?".

      COVERAGE, measured on S32K344 and not a general Zephyr property: this SoC
      zeroes SRAM and both TCMs after a DESTRUCTIVE reset, below Zephyr, because
      ECC memory must be written by a 64-bit master first. A record therefore
      survives a functional reset (SYSRESETREQ) and NOT a destructive one
      (power-on, pin reset). Closing that gap needs the flash write to happen in
      the fault handler rather than at the next boot; see the file comment.

config NROS_FAULT_LOG_FLASH
    bool "Also persist the fault record to the storage flash partition"
    depends on NROS_FAULT_LOG && FLASH && FLASH_MAP
    default n
    help
      Copy the record to flash at the next boot, from thread context where a
      flash write is safe and a failure can be reported. `.noinit` already
      survives the reset a fault produces; this adds survival across a power
      cycle, and costs no SRAM.

      Uses one slot at the start of `storage_partition`. A ring buffer would be
      a second thing to get wrong while debugging the first.

config NROS_ZENOH_AUTO_RECONNECT
    bool "zenoh-pico reopens the session automatically when the transport fails"
    default y
    help
      Maps to Z_FEATURE_AUTO_RECONNECT, which zenoh-pico defaults to 1. Until
      issue 0882 there was no way for an image to say otherwise.

      Turning it OFF is a diagnostic and a mitigation. The reconnect runs inside
      `_zp_unicast_failed`, on the lease task, AFTER
      `_z_common_transport_clear` has destroyed the transport's mutexes and
      freed the lease task's own handle. On this board that reliably ends in

          ASSERTION FAIL [z_spin_lock_valid(l)]  Invalid spinlock

      with the offending lock inside the nano-ros heap — a mutex used after the
      allocation holding it was released.

      With this off, a transport failure ends the session instead of trying to
      rebuild it under itself. That is a real loss of function: nothing
      re-establishes the link. Prefer fixing the ordering upstream; this exists
      so an image can choose a clean stop over an unsafe reopen while that
      fix is pending.

config NROS_ZEPHYR_STACKS_IN_DTCM
    bool "Place nano-ros task stacks in DTCM"
    default n
    help
      Put the nano-ros task stack array in the SoC's data tightly-coupled
      memory instead of SRAM, using the linker region the devicetree's
      `zephyr,memory-region = "DTCM"` generates.

      Off by default because a DTCM is a property of the part, not of Zephyr:
      a SoC without one, or one whose DTCM another component already claims,
      must keep the default placement. Enabling it on a board with no DTCM
      region is a link error, which is the right failure.

      Stacks are the ideal tenant. They are CPU-private, never the target of a
      DMA transfer, and TCM access does not contend with the system bus — so
      this is a latency improvement as well as an SRAM saving. On the
      MR-CANHUBK344 the array is 48 KiB against 128 KiB of DTCM that was
      reporting 0 %% while SRAM sat at 98.7 %%.

      Do NOT extend this to buffers a DMA engine touches without checking
      whether this part's TCM is reachable from the relevant bus master. On
      Cortex-M7 the TCMs hang off the CPU's private bus and typically are not.

config NROS_ZEPHYR_TASK_SLOTS
    int "nano-ros Zephyr task stack slots"
    default 4
    help
      NROS_ZEPHYR_MAX_THREADS — how many task stacks are reserved. The index
      into them only ever RISES: `nros_zephyr_task_create` bounds-checks and
      returns -1 when they run out, but nothing releases a slot when a task
      ends. A workload that recreates tasks (any reconnect) therefore consumes
      these permanently. Total cost is
      NROS_ZEPHYR_TASK_SLOTS x NROS_ZEPHYR_TASK_STACK_SIZE.

config NROS_ZENOH_LEASE_MS
    int "zenoh-pico transport lease (ms)"
    default 10000
    help
      Z_TRANSPORT_LEASE — the session lease zenoh-pico announces, and the
      value its lease task measures peer silence against. It is BOTH sides
      of one number: keepalives go out every lease/NROS_ZENOH_LEASE_FACTOR,
      and a peer is dropped after TWO lease periods with nothing received.

      Raise this on a link whose peer does not send keepalives. Measured
      against a stock rmw_zenohd over UART: the router completes the
      handshake, answers the declares, then schedules nothing further on the
      link — so a publisher, which has no return traffic to reset its lease,
      closes with _Z_CLOSE_EXPIRED at exactly 2 x this value. At the 10000
      default that is a 20 s session, which is long enough for discovery to
      succeed sometimes and look like a flake. 60000 gives 120 s.

      This is mitigation. The peer is what should be keepaliving; a longer
      lease only delays the drop and slows genuine peer-loss detection by
      the same factor.

config NROS_ZENOH_LEASE_FACTOR
    int "zenoh-pico lease expire factor"
    default 3
    help
      Z_TRANSPORT_LEASE_EXPIRE_FACTOR — divides NROS_ZENOH_LEASE_MS to give
      the keepalive interval. 3 means one keepalive every lease/3, so three
      per lease period. Lower it to send keepalives more often against a
      peer with a short lease of its own.

config NROS_ZENOH_READ_PRIORITY
    int "zenoh-pico read task priority (normalised band, 0-255)"
    default 200
    help
      Scheduling priority of the zenoh-pico READ task, on the platform ABI's
      normalised band: 0 = least urgent, NROS_PLATFORM_PRIORITY_MAX (255) =
      most urgent (phase-364 W5). nros_zephyr_native_priority maps it onto
      whatever sched_get_priority_min/max report for SCHED_RR in THIS image,
      which depends on CONFIG_NUM_PREEMPT_PRIORITIES.

      issue 0852 — THE SCALE CHANGED, AND THE OLD VALUES DO NOT CARRY OVER.
      This was documented "normalized 0-31" with a default of 16, and mapped by
      a second, private conversion (zpico_posix_set_priority) that wrote into a
      pthread_attr_t nothing on this platform ever read. Two normalised bands
      meeting in one scheduler is issue 0623 verbatim: a 16 authored against
      0-31 is (16*14)/255 = 0 through the real map — the LEAST urgent slot,
      which is exactly where every Zephyr transport task has been running.
      The private scale and its conversion are deleted; this band is the only
      one left.

      Why 200: on a CONFIG_NUM_PREEMPT_PRIORITIES=15 image it maps to SCHED_RR
      10, i.e. k_thread 4 — ABOVE the tier pool ([8,14] in
      [board.priority_plan]) and below the main thread. That ordering is
      CHOSEN, not inherited: it is what the board's priority plan already
      declares, and what FreeRTOS ships (app 3 / transport 4). The reverse
      starves the RX drain and stalls publishers on retransmission timeouts
      (issue 0623). Both orderings are legitimate; choosing by accident is the
      bug this issue was.

      The number is IMAGE-DERIVED (issue 0766): re-resolve it against your
      own .config rather than assuming k_thread 4.

      Before issue 0626 this was not settable at all on Zephyr: the shim's
      POSIX branch configured only the stack size, carrying a comment about
      SCHED_FIFO needing root — true of Linux, meaningless on Zephyr — so the
      transport tasks ran at whatever default applied and issue 0506's
      question (what preempts the RT tiers under load?) could not be asked.

      Raising it above your RT tiers means transport preempts control;
      lowering it below means a busy tier can starve the RX drain, which
      stalls publishers on retransmission timeouts. Both are real failure
      modes with real cost — see issues 0506 and 0623 before changing it.

config NROS_ZENOH_LEASE_PRIORITY
    int "zenoh-pico lease task priority (normalised band, 0-255)"
    default 200
    help
      As NROS_ZENOH_READ_PRIORITY, for the lease (keep-alive) task. The lease
      task is light but latency-sensitive: if it cannot run, the session's
      keep-alives stop and the router drops the peer, which presents as an
      unexplained disconnect rather than as a scheduling problem.

config NROS_ZENOH_TX_BATCH
    bool "zenoh-pico tx batching (one send per executor spin)"
    # DERIVED from nros-platform-zephyr [knobs.zenoh.tx].batch —
    # re-render with `scripts/check-zephyr-knob-agreement.py --write`.
    default y
    help
      phase-279 (#145) — tx batching (ZPICO_TX_BATCH). Publishes queue
      in the transport write buffer and ship as ONE socket send per executor
      spin (zpico_spin_once flush), so total tx throughput scales with
      messages-per-flush instead of being capped at ~1 send per socket recv
      window (the zsock per-fd-mutex ceiling above). Adds up to FLUSH_MS of
      publish latency; service/query requests and replies bypass the batch
      (express), as do publishers declared express (tx_express QoS).
      phase-290 (RFC-0049) — DEFAULT ON for Zephyr: the phase-282 promotion
      (option C), measured 15-20x streaming on the only platform with the
      per-fd tx ceiling. Requires the issue-0213 fork fix (declarations
      bypass the batch — otherwise a server's readiness banner outruns its
      still-batched queryable declares and immediately-fired queries miss).
      This default MUST mirror
      config/zephyr/nros-platform.toml [knobs.zenoh.tx] — the
      kconfig_platform_default_drift test asserts the mirror.

config NROS_ZENOH_TX_SPLIT_LOCK
    bool "zenoh-pico split tx locking (append while sending)"
    # DERIVED from nros-platform-zephyr [knobs.zenoh.tx].split_lock —
    # re-render with `scripts/check-zephyr-knob-agreement.py --write`.
    default y
    depends on NROS_ZENOH_TX_BATCH
    help
      phase-282 (#145) — the batch flush steals the pending write buffer under
      the tx mutex and performs the socket write under a separate link-write
      mutex, so publishers keep appending to the fresh batch while the (up to
      one recv-window) send is in flight. Wire order still equals SN order.
      Requires TX_BATCH. Costs a second wbuf (Z_BATCH_UNICAST_SIZE) of RAM
      per session. phase-290 — DEFAULT ON for Zephyr (promotion option C);
      mirrors config/zephyr/nros-platform.toml (drift-tested).

config NROS_ZENOH_TX_BATCH_FLUSH_MS
    int "zenoh-pico tx batch flush cadence (ms)"
    # DERIVED from nros-platform-zephyr [knobs.zenoh.tx].flush_ms —
    # re-render with `scripts/check-zephyr-knob-agreement.py --write`.
    default 50
    depends on NROS_ZENOH_TX_BATCH
    help
      phase-282 (#145) — period of the dedicated tx-flush thread (and the
      rate limit of the spin-driven fallback flush) when TX_BATCH is on.
      Bounds the extra publish latency batching adds: a batched sample waits
      at most this long before it ships (express publishers bypass the batch
      entirely). Lower = less added latency, more (smaller) socket sends.
      Forwards to zpico.c's ZPICO_TX_BATCH_FLUSH_MS.

config NROS_ZENOH_PUBLICATION
    bool "Publication support"
    default y
    help
      Enable zenoh-pico publication support. Required for publishers.
      Maps to Z_FEATURE_PUBLICATION.

config NROS_ZENOH_SUBSCRIPTION
    bool "Subscription support"
    default y
    help
      Enable zenoh-pico subscription support. Required for subscribers.
      Maps to Z_FEATURE_SUBSCRIPTION.

config NROS_ZENOH_QUERY
    bool "Query support (service client)"
    default y
    help
      Enable zenoh-pico query support. Required for service clients
      and action clients.
      Maps to Z_FEATURE_QUERY.

config NROS_ZENOH_QUERYABLE
    bool "Queryable support (service server)"
    default y
    help
      Enable zenoh-pico queryable support. Required for service servers
      and action servers.
      Maps to Z_FEATURE_QUERYABLE.

config NROS_ZENOH_LINK_TCP
    bool "TCP link"
    depends on NET_SOCKETS
    default y
    help
      Enable TCP transport link. This is the primary transport for
      connecting to a zenoh router.
      Maps to Z_FEATURE_LINK_TCP.

      phase-400 W5 — `depends on NET_SOCKETS` MIRRORS the resolver rule
      `transport.kind=tcp requires ip_stack` (RFC-0086 D2). Without it this
      symbol stayed `y` under CONFIG_NETWORKING=n and the build died in
      zenoh-pico on AF_INET / socklen_t / struct sockaddr — a config error
      surfacing as a link error two layers down. The rule LIVES in the
      resolver, because the cargo and CMake lanes have no `depends on`; this
      is the Zephyr projection of it, held by the drift test.

config NROS_ZENOH_LINK_UDP_UNICAST
    bool "UDP unicast link"
    depends on NET_SOCKETS
    default n
    help
      Enable UDP unicast transport link.
      Maps to Z_FEATURE_LINK_UDP_UNICAST.

config NROS_ZENOH_LINK_UDP_MULTICAST
    bool "UDP multicast link"
    depends on NET_SOCKETS
    default n
    help
      Enable UDP multicast transport link.
      Maps to Z_FEATURE_LINK_UDP_MULTICAST.

config NROS_ZENOH_SCOUTING
    bool "Scouting (UDP discovery)"
    depends on NET_SOCKETS
    default n
    help
      Enable scouting for automatic router discovery via UDP.
      Maps to Z_FEATURE_SCOUTING.

config NROS_ZENOH_LINK_SERIAL
    bool "Serial/UART link"
    depends on SERIAL
    default n
    help
      Enable serial/UART transport link.
      Maps to Z_FEATURE_LINK_SERIAL.

config NROS_ZENOH_LINK_CAN
    bool "CAN / CAN FD link"
    default n
    help
      Enable CAN transport for zenoh-pico (RFC-0080). The link is a
      DATAGRAM link with a 63-byte MTU on CAN FD (64-byte frame minus a
      length byte) or 7 bytes on classic CAN; zenoh's own transport
      fragments anything larger, so no segmentation layer is involved.

      Endpoint syntax:
        can/<device>#bitrate=500000;dbitrate=2000000;tx_id=0x100;rx_id=0x101

      Requires a CAN controller (CONFIG_CAN) on the board.
      Maps to Z_FEATURE_LINK_CAN.

config NROS_ZENOH_LINK_ISOTP
    bool "CAN unicast over ISO-TP"
    default n
    select ISOTP
    help
      Enable CAN unicast for zenoh-pico over ISO 15765-2 (RFC-0083),
      using Zephyr's own subsys/canbus/isotp rather than a vendored
      implementation.

      This is a UNICAST link, unlike NROS_ZENOH_LINK_CAN. zenoh routes
      queries and liveliness to unicast faces only, so this is the link
      that carries ROS services, actions, parameters and graph
      introspection over CAN; the multicast CAN link carries topics and
      nothing built on a query. Segmentation also moves below zenoh, so
      the MTU is 4095 bytes rather than 63 -- which is what makes
      CLASSIC CAN usable, and classic CAN is most of the hardware in
      the field.

      A peer is addressed by a DIRECTED IDENTIFIER PAIR: this node's
      tx_id must be the other node's rx_id and vice versa. Exactly one
      peer may own the other end of a pair.

      Endpoint syntax:
        isotp/<device>#tx_id=0x7E0;rx_id=0x7E8

      Requires a CAN controller (CONFIG_CAN) on the board. Zephyr marks
      CONFIG_ISOTP [EXPERIMENTAL]; its own conformance and
      implementation suites pass on native_sim, with the STmin cases
      skipped -- see docs/roadmap/archived/phase-394-can-unicast-for-zenoh-pico.md.
      Maps to Z_FEATURE_LINK_ISOTP.

config NROS_ZENOH_LINK_WS
    bool "WebSocket link"
    default n
    help
      Enable WebSocket transport link.
      Maps to Z_FEATURE_LINK_WS.

config NROS_ZENOH_RAWETH_TRANSPORT
    bool "Raw Ethernet transport"
    default n
    help
      Enable raw Ethernet transport (bypasses IP stack).
      Maps to Z_FEATURE_RAWETH_TRANSPORT.

