config NROS_MAX_PENDING_GETS
    int "Maximum concurrent pending get operations"
    default 4
    help
      Maximum number of concurrent pending get (service call) operations.
      Maps to ZPICO_MAX_PENDING_GETS.

config NROS_GET_REPLY_BUF_SIZE
    int "Get reply buffer size"
    default 4096
    help
      Size of the buffer for get (service call) reply payloads (bytes).
      Maps to ZPICO_GET_REPLY_BUF_SIZE.

config NROS_GRAPH_CACHE_SIZE
    int "Graph liveliness cache size"
    default 65536
    help
      Bytes of NUL-separated liveliness keyexprs the graph cache holds.
      Maps to ZPICO_GRAPH_CACHE_SIZE.

      The 65536 default is sized for a HOST-scale domain: upstream measured a
      box running one stock talker plus the ROS 2 daemon carrying 222 tokens,
      about 31 KB, at roughly 140 bytes per keyexpr.

      That default is 20% of this board's entire SRAM, and it lands in .bss
      whether or not the graph is ever queried. An image whose domain holds a
      handful of entities should turn it down: 8192 still holds ~58 tokens.
      Overflow is counted and reported by zpico_graph_cache_copy, never
      silently truncated, so a value that is too small shows up as a drop
      count rather than as a wrong answer.

config NROS_GET_POLL_INTERVAL_MS
    int "Get poll interval (ms)"
    default 10
    help
      Polling interval for pending get operations (milliseconds).
      Maps to ZPICO_GET_POLL_INTERVAL_MS.

config NROS_FRAG_MAX_SIZE
    int "Maximum reassembled message size"
    default 2048
    help
      Maximum reassembled message size after defragmentation (bytes).
      Maps to ZPICO_FRAG_MAX_SIZE.

config NROS_BATCH_UNICAST_SIZE
    int "Maximum unicast batch size"
    default 1024
    help
      Maximum unicast batch size before fragmentation (bytes).
      Maps to ZPICO_BATCH_UNICAST_SIZE.

config NROS_SUBSCRIBER_BUFFER_SIZE
    int "Per-subscriber static buffer size (-1 = derive)"
    default -1
    help
      Size of the static buffer for each subscriber slot (bytes) -- the SMALL
      zenoh payload class. Maps to NROS_SUBSCRIBER_BUFFER_SIZE (one name: the executor arena reads it too, phase-403).

      -1 means DERIVE it from this image's message-bound inventory
      (phase-403 W8 / issue 0940): the largest derived bound at or under the
      small/large class split (ZPICO_SUBSCRIBER_SIZE_THRESHOLD, 2048 by
      default; it has no Kconfig row). Any other value is yours and WINS.

      READ THIS BEFORE TRUSTING THE DERIVED NUMBER. The inventory holds every
      type in the LINKED interface closure, not only the ones you subscribe to,
      so the derived size is an UPPER BOUND: too big, never too small. On the
      mr-canhubk344 island it is 1496 B, set by std_msgs/Float64MultiArray,
      against 880 B for the largest type the image actually receives -- the
      difference is the price of having no ENTITY inventory (phase-403 W4).
      The configure prints which type set the number; if it is one you do not
      receive, state your own value here.

      If ANY type in the closure has no derived bound, NOTHING is derived and
      the crate default (1024) applies -- the configure says so and names the
      types. Deriving over the bounded subset would publish a maximum a real
      sample can exceed, which is a SILENT drop on the C++ arena path.

config NROS_SUBSCRIBER_RING_DEPTH
    int "Receive ring depth per subscriber"
    default 4
    help
      Samples held per subscriber before the ring drops the oldest. This is
      the receive queue depth, so lowering it is a QoS reduction, not slack.
      It multiplies BOTH zenoh payload classes:
        SMALL_PAYLOADS = ZPICO_MAX_SUBSCRIBERS x DEPTH x SUBSCRIBER_BUFFER_SIZE
        LARGE_PAYLOADS = MAX_LARGE_SUBSCRIBERS x DEPTH x SUBSCRIBER_LARGE_SIZE
      Maps to ZPICO_SUBSCRIBER_RING_DEPTH.

config NROS_MAX_LARGE_SUBSCRIBERS
    int "Subscribers served from the large payload class (-1 = derive)"
    default -1
    help
      Subscribers whose rx_buffer_hint exceeds SUBSCRIBER_SIZE_THRESHOLD and
      are therefore served from LARGE_PAYLOADS. Bounded separately from
      ZPICO_MAX_SUBSCRIBERS so raising SUBSCRIBER_LARGE_SIZE for one
      image-scale topic does not multiply across every subscriber.
      Maps to ZPICO_MAX_LARGE_SUBSCRIBERS.

      -1 means DERIVE it: how many types in the linked interface closure
      exceed the class split. This is the number issue 0940 records a human
      producing by reading generated C++ headers BY EYE -- headers that state
      an ESTIMATE and not a bound (phase-403 W6), which is how the island got
      2 where the derived answer is 0.

      0 is a legal, meaningful value and NOT the same as -1: it claims that
      every type this image receives fits the small class, and the large pool
      becomes zero bytes instead of RING_DEPTH x LARGE_SIZE. A hint no class
      can hold is then refused at create_subscription rather than served a
      block too small for it.

      Derived counts TYPES, not subscriptions -- an upper bound, since the
      closure is what is linked, not what is subscribed. See
      NROS_SUBSCRIBER_BUFFER_SIZE for the rest of that caveat.

config NROS_SUBSCRIBER_LARGE_SIZE
    int "Large-class payload buffer size (-1 = derive)"
    default -1
    help
      Per-sample capacity in the large payload class (bytes). The crate default
      is 16384, sized for image-scale messages; an image whose largest
      subscribed type is a few hundred bytes pays 2 x 4 x 16384 = 128 KiB for
      nothing. Maps to ZPICO_SUBSCRIBER_LARGE_SIZE.

      -1 means DERIVE it: the largest derived bound above the class split.
      When NO type is above the split the size is deliberately NOT derived --
      with zero blocks the pool is zero bytes whatever size it names, and
      naming one for a class that does not exist would be inventing a number.
      The crate default then applies and costs nothing.

config NROS_SERVICE_BUFFER_SIZE
    int "Per-service static buffer size"
    default 1024
    help
      Size of the static buffer for each service server slot (bytes).
      Maps to ZPICO_SERVICE_BUFFER_SIZE.

# =============================================================================
# Zenoh Platform Configuration
# =============================================================================

config NROS_ZENOH_LOCATOR
    string "Zenoh router locator"
    default "tcp/127.0.0.1:7447"
    help
      Zenoh router address. Supported formats:
        TCP:    "tcp/IP:PORT"       (e.g. "tcp/127.0.0.1:7447")
        Serial: "serial/DEVICE#baudrate=RATE"

      7447 is the port `rmw_zenoh_cpp` uses on BOTH sides of a ROS 2
      deployment: it is what `ros2 run rmw_zenoh_cpp rmw_zenohd` listens on
      and what a `rmw_zenoh_cpp` node connects to, and it is what our own
      launcher defaults to (`just zenohd` -> `nros_router_exec
      "${ZENOH_LOCATOR:-tcp/127.0.0.1:7447}"`, `nros_router_hint`).

      This default was 7456, which is nothing's default but this line's. The
      two halves of our OWN documented workflow therefore disagreed: a user
      who ran `just zenohd` and then their Zephyr image got

          executor open failed: Transport(ConnectionFailed)

      with a router running the whole time, and a `ros2 topic echo` against
      the 7456 router got "Unable to connect to a Zenoh router" from the ROS
      side for the mirror-image reason. Neither message names a port.

      Safe to move because a fixture that needs an isolated router ALLOCATES
      one and passes it explicitly (`west_zenoh_locator`, or the role-leaf
      formula in `scripts/build/zephyr-fixture-leaves.sh` -> a
      `-DCONFIG_NROS_ZENOH_LOCATOR` define), so this default is what a USER
      gets, not what the matrix runs on. Measured over the built tree when
      this changed: of 35 zephyr images carrying a locator, 34 set their own
      and 1 (`build-logging-smoke`, which publishes nothing) rode the
      default. No fixture claims 7447.

config NROS_TRANSPORT_SERIAL
    bool "Enable serial/UART transport"
    select NROS_ZENOH_LINK_SERIAL
    help
      Enable serial/UART transport for zenoh-pico.

endif # NROS_RMW_ZENOH

