# C API executor limits are derived from the Rust executor limits below.
# NROS_EXECUTOR_MAX_CBS controls the total handle count for both APIs.

# =============================================================================
# Executor Limits
# =============================================================================

config NROS_EXECUTOR_MAX_CBS
    int "Maximum executor callback slots (-1 = derive)"
    default -1
    help
      Maximum number of callback slots in the executor
      (subscriptions + timers + services + service clients + action
      entities + guard conditions). Controls both Rust and C APIs.
      Arena size is derived automatically from this value and the
      subscription buffer size.

      A PUBLISHER IS NOT ONE OF THEM. Every registration that claims a slot
      calls Executor::next_entry_slot(); create_publisher does not — it writes
      an RmwPublisher into caller-owned storage and there is no
      nros_executor_add_publisher on the C path. The mr-canhubk344 bring-up
      counted 33 "handles" for the island and set 36 here; 14 of those 33 are
      publishers and the slot demand is 19.

      -1 means DERIVE it from this image's ENTITY inventory (phase-403 W9 /
      issue 0965): the sum of every `nano_ros_node_register(... ENTITIES ...)`
      declaration in the image, counted by kind. It is derived only when EVERY
      component declared; one that did not makes the whole image refuse, so a
      partial answer can never be published as a whole one. Any value other
      than -1 is yours and WINS; the crate default when nothing is stated and
      nothing is derivable is 4.

      The derived value carries NO headroom, which makes the running image a
      checker of its own declaration: one entity past the table and
      registration returns ExecutorFull naming this knob, and the entry halts
      boot naming the node. Raise it deliberately if you want slack — the
      arena scales with it.

      4 is the crate default in nros-node/build.rs. This option was never
      forwarded to Cargo before issue 0316, so every Zephyr C image compiled
      that default regardless of what was configured here; the old default of
      16 had therefore never been built. Honouring it as-is would have grown a
      native_sim listener's .bss by 219 KB (+35%) as a side effect of repairing
      the plumbing.

config NROS_SUBSCRIPTION_BUFFER_SIZE
    int "Per-subscription receive buffer size in bytes (-1 = derive)"
    default -1
    help
      Serialized-sample capacity of every executor arena subscription
      (nros-node build.rs DEFAULT_RX_BUF_SIZE). A sample larger than this
      is dropped at take with BUFFER_TOO_SMALL — for the C++ arena
      dispatch path that drop is silent, so an undersized buffer looks
      like a subscription that never fires. Size it to the largest
      serialized message the image subscribes to (e.g. an Autoware
      /planning trajectory runs 9-14 KiB). The executor arena is derived
      from this value times the callback-slot count — raise it
      deliberately. This knob was resolvable only via environment on
      native lanes before; the Zephyr lane's curated cargo environment
      dropped it, so every Zephyr image silently compiled 1024
      (same class as issue 0316 / the NROS_EXECUTOR_MAX_CBS history).

      -1 means DERIVE it from this image's message-bound inventory
      (phase-403 W8 / issue 0940): the largest derived bound in the LINKED
      interface closure, which is an UPPER BOUND on what the image needs and
      never an under-estimate. "Size it to the largest serialized message the
      image subscribes to" is exactly the instruction nobody could follow by
      hand -- the island's board conf reasons its way to ~718 B for
      nav_msgs/Odometry, writes 512, and the derived bound is 880. Any value
      other than -1 is yours and WINS; the crate default when nothing is
      stated and nothing is derivable is 1024.

      COUPLED TO THE ARENA: the arena is max_cbs * (3 * this + 512) + 2048, so
      raising this raises the arena. An image that also PINS
      NROS_EXECUTOR_ARENA_SIZE must move the two together; leaving both to
      derive is the shape that cannot disagree.

config NROS_BOOT_REPORT
    bool "Write a boot self-report into RAM"
    default n
    help
      The image keeps a fixed 60-byte record in RAM describing itself: the
      knobs it was COMPILED with, how far boot got, and the arena allocation
      that did not fit. Read it back with a debugger after halting the core:

        python3 scripts/read-boot-report.py build/zephyr/zephyr.elf dump.bin

      For boards where no log sink reaches a human. On the MR-CANHUBK344 the
      console UART is not wired and the second UART carries the zenoh serial
      transport, so both of issue 0900's arena diagnostics -- the two messages
      written specifically to explain an arena failure -- go nowhere.

      Unlike a log, this survives the failure it reports. An under-sized arena
      halts DURING entity creation, before the first spin, so the advisory
      never prints; the record's last stage and failed allocation are still
      there to be read.

      OFF by default: an image that does not enable it is byte-identical to one
      built before the feature existed.

config NROS_EXECUTOR_MAX_SC
    int "Maximum scheduling-context slots per executor"
    default 8
    help
      SchedContext slots per executor (nros-node build.rs MAX_SC,
      RFC-0052 / Phase 110.B).

config NROS_EXECUTOR_MAX_SHUTDOWN_CBS
    int "Shutdown-hook slots per phase"
    default 2
    help
      How many shutdown hooks the executor can hold PER PHASE — one table
      this size for pre-shutdown hooks (run while the session is still open,
      so a node can publish a final state or park an actuator) and a second
      for on-shutdown hooks (run after the close). Issue 0790;
      nros-node/build.rs NROS_EXECUTOR_MAX_SHUTDOWN_CBS.

      Deliberately small: the slots are static, so every image pays for them
      whether or not it registers a hook (the same cost issue 0460 measured
      for queryable slots). Raise it only when an image genuinely has more
      things to park on the way down. Overflow is reported at registration
      (NROS_RET_FULL), not silently dropped.

config NROS_EXECUTOR_MAX_NODES
    int "Maximum nodes per executor (-1 = derive)"
    default -1
    help
      Nodes attachable to one executor (nros-node build.rs MAX_NODES,
      Phase 104.C.2).

config NROS_PARAM_SERVICE_BUFFER_SIZE
    int "Parameter service request/reply buffer size (bytes)"
    default 4096
    help
      Buffer for parameter service requests/replies (nros-node build.rs
      PARAM_SERVICE_BUFFER_SIZE).

config NROS_RMW_SUBSCRIBER_SLOTS
    int "Static subscription-handle slots (no_std targets) (-1 = derive)"
    default -1
    help
      nros-rmw-cffi keeps subscription handles in a static slot pool on
      no_std targets, since there is no allocator to Box them into. The
      Nth+1 create_subscription returns BAD_ALLOC and the executor surfaces
      it as an opaque SubscriberCreationFailed, so this must be at least the
      number of subscriptions the image creates. Pool size is this x 1024
      bytes. Read by nros-rmw-cffi build.rs.

      phase-412 W1 -- `-1` DERIVES this from the image's entity inventory
      (`nano_ros_node_register(... ENTITIES ...)`). Stating a number here WINS
      and suppresses the derivation, which is a fine thing to do deliberately
      and a silent cost otherwise.

      The derived value counts what the SESSION opens, which is not what the
      author declares: a declared action server is one entity that costs three
      queryables and two publishers, and an action client one subscription.
      Those multipliers live beside the calls that decide them.

      NOT counted, because a feature enables them and the inventory cannot see
      it: the parameter (6) and lifecycle (5) service families. An image
      carrying either must state this knob.

config NROS_EXECUTOR_ACTION_CLIENTS
    int "Callback slots to budget at ActionClient size (-1 = derive)"
    default -1
    help
      How many of NROS_EXECUTOR_MAX_CBS slots the arena derivation budgets at
      ACTION CLIENT size (~18 KiB each) rather than pub/sub size (~3.5 KiB).

      -1 DERIVES it from the image's entity inventory, the same way MAX_CBS is
      derived: the count is the action clients AND action servers the image's
      components declare. An image whose inventory refuses -- any component
      that declared no ENTITIES -- falls through to MAX_CBS, which reproduces
      the historical arithmetic exactly, so nothing built before this moves.

      Action SERVERS count too, though the option is named for clients: the
      arena stores ActionServerArenaEntry, so a server image occupies the same
      heavy slots and zeroing this on one would trade the saving for a runtime
      BufferTooSmall.

      Set it to 0 on an image with no action client: at the defaults that takes
      the arena from 74240 bytes to 16384. The arena is INLINE ON THE TASK
      STACK, not in .bss, so this is stack headroom -- see the FreeRTOS
      "Invalid mbox" note in docs/reference/platform-implementation-notes.md.

      Too small fails at REGISTRATION with BufferTooSmall, not at link. The
      executor reports what it actually used on its first spin, so build once,
      read the advisory, then set this. Issue 0900.

config NROS_ZEPHYR_HEAP_SIZE
    int "Application heap arena size (bytes)"
    default 65536
    help
      Size of the rlsf arena in nros-platform's zephyr_heap, which backs
      BOTH z_malloc and __rust_alloc since the allocation funnel moved off
      Zephyr's kernel heap.

      CONFIG_HEAP_MEM_POOL_SIZE does NOT govern application allocation any
      more; this does. An application that outgrows the arena does not
      fault or log -- it stops, so size this to what the application
      actually needs rather than leaving the default in place and
      inferring from a hang.

      An NROS_ZEPHYR_HEAP_SIZE environment variable overrides this value.

config NROS_EXECUTOR_ARENA_SIZE
    int "Executor arena size in bytes (0 = derive)"
    default 0
    help
      Explicit executor arena size. 0 means let nros-node build.rs derive it,
      which budgets EVERY callback slot at action-client size (~18 KiB) --
      correct, but an order of magnitude over for a pub/sub-only image, which
      needs about max_cbs * (3 * NROS_SUBSCRIPTION_BUFFER_SIZE + 512) + 2048.
      Set it only with that arithmetic in hand; too small fails at runtime,
      not at link.

config NROS_EXECUTOR_BACKING_U64S
    int "Executor backing reservation in 64-bit words (-1 = derive, 0 = none)"
    default -1
    help
      How many 64-bit words nros-node reserves in .bss for the executor's
      per-entry storage -- the named static
      nros_node::executor::backing::EXECUTOR_BACKING (phase-392 W6,
      RFC-0002 SS 4.4b). The reservation is exactly 8 x this many bytes, on
      every target.

      -1 DERIVES it, from ExecutorSizing::DEFAULT: MAX_CBS, MAX_SC, MAX_NODES
      and the arena. That is the right answer for an image that does not care
      which budget the bytes come out of.

      STATE A NUMBER when the image lowers CONFIG_COMMON_LIBC_MALLOC_ARENA_SIZE
      to pay for the reservation (issue 1145). The Rust global allocator here is
      picolibc malloc, whose arena is ITSELF a fixed static, and before W6 the
      backing was a Box::leak out of it -- so leaving that arena where it was
      reserves the same bytes twice. Lowering it by a number copied out of `nm`
      pairs them once and then drifts the next time an executor knob moves,
      in whichever direction, with nothing to say so (issue 1171).

      Stating the size inverts that. The crate obeys this number, so the
      pairing is exact by construction and identical on every board -- the
      DERIVED size is target-dependent (87,256 B on mps2_an385 against
      88,328 B on native_sim/native/64 for one and the same conf, which is how
      1145's hand-paired leaf kept double-reserving 1,072 bytes on the second
      board) and 8 x this is not. State it too SMALL and nros-node refuses to
      compile naming this knob, which is the direction that would otherwise
      silently re-create the double reservation.

      A conf that states this must lower the arena by exactly 8 x this many
      bytes and record what it lowered it FROM:

          # nros-arena-base: 1048576
          CONFIG_NROS_EXECUTOR_BACKING_U64S=11041
          CONFIG_COMMON_LIBC_MALLOC_ARENA_SIZE=960248

      check-executor-backing-arena-pairing holds every conf to that arithmetic.

      0 declines the static entirely and restores the pre-W6 leak, for an image
      that would rather keep the bytes in the allocator arena than have the
      linker price them.

config NROS_MAX_PARAMETERS
    int "Maximum declared parameters per image"
    default 32
    help
      Static parameter-store pool size (nros-params build.rs). A node
      declaring more parameters than this fails declare_parameter with
      ErrorCode::Full (-5) at boot. The Autoware trajectory follower
      declares ~150.

